- op: discuss  # 9a1a
  at: '2026-08-11T17:14:52Z'  # 9a1a
  by: agent  # 9a1a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9a1a
  clock: 3  # 9a1a
  nonce: 9a1a  # 9a1a
  message: "EXECUTED. Result, method revisions and the full verdict table are in\ndocs/measurements/0001-taint-flow-precision.md; the instrument is\nscripts/measure-taint-precision.py and the claims are\ndocs/example-claims/generic-taint-claims.yaml. Numbers below; reasoning in the record.\n\nRESULT: ~41% population-weighted precision. Two populations, both adjudicated by\nreading source against a rubric fixed in advance:\n\n  CENSUS  every flow on caddy / mitmproxy / poetry / express / apollo-server\n          60 flows -> 42 adjudicated, 11 TP, 31 FP = 26.2%, 18 UNADJUDICABLE\n  SAMPLE  pretix, stratified 44 of 205\n          43 adjudicated, 17 TP, 26 FP = 39.5% (population-weighted 44.1%)\n  combined 28 of 85 = 32.9% raw; ~41% weighted by population\n\nBAND, named before any interval was computed exactly as this item requires: <50%.\nThis item's own consequence for that band is \"precision is the headline problem and\nrecall work should stop until it is understood\". The mechanisms are now understood\nand named -- see below -- so the condition is a decision for the owner rather than a\ngate that fires by itself. Nothing in the data supports a number near the 80%\nthreshold at which this item said WI-kabif would become arguable.\n\nWHERE THE UNCERTAINTY IS: the census has NO sampling error (it is every flow those\nfive repos produce). The pretix figure is a sample with a 95% interval of 25.1-54.0%\nand cannot by itself exclude 50%.\n\nFOUR REVISIONS, recorded here as this item requires:\n 1. POPULATION. The pre-registered frame -- \"the 1,890 flows on hypergumbo's two\n    violated self-claims\" -- NO LONGER EXISTS. Both claims were repaired since this\n    item was written, and a repaired claim reports zero violating flows because\n    verify_claims never downgrades a violated verdict, only a would-be confirmed one.\n    Verified live, not by proxy: verify-claims on hypergumbo returns 18 verdicts, all\n    inconclusive. The frame is empty by construction and the WI-vanun park is moot.\n 2. DESIGN. Census-plus-sample rather than one n=60 stratified sample, because five\n    of the six repos produce 60 flows in total and a census has no sampling error.\n 3. SAMPLE SIZE. pretix drawn at target n=30 / per-cell floor 3 (44 flows). The\n    pre-registered floor of 10 over pretix's 10 strata would have drawn 120+, which\n    is not adjudicable at the care this rubric demands. Fixed before any pretix flow\n    was labelled; the per-stratum counts were already visible from collection.\n 4. WHO ADJUDICATES. The census used the pre-registered method (me, then a blind\n    verifier, then a third agent on disagreement). For pretix I replaced my own pass\n    with a SECOND independent blind agent and kept only the tie-break. That\n    deliberately reduces the one bias this item named and could not control -- \"I\n    wrote much of the code being measured\" -- and it is the only revision that makes\n    the method stronger rather than smaller.\n\nDISAGREEMENT RATE: 2 of 104 = 1.9%. Both were the same question (does taint flow\nthrough a mutating call?) and resolving them consistently needed a principle the\nrubric had not stated: TAINT FLOWS THROUGH IN-PROGRAM COMPUTATION, NOT THROUGH AN\nEXTERNAL RESOURCE SELECTED BY THE TAINTED VALUE. An argv-derived filename that is\nopened is FP; an ORM row rendered onto a canvas whose buffer is then written is TP.\nStated in the record: a low disagreement rate shows the rubric is applied\nconsistently, NOT that it is right -- every pass shares it, and its\ncontrol-dependence convention moves the headline 8.3pp on its own.\n\nTHE FINDING THAT MATTERS MOST, because it refutes the remedy the data invites:\nanalysis_method REVERSES between populations. ddg is 100% on both (4/4, 3/3), but\nddg_mixed is 11.8% on the census and 57.9% on pretix. Filtering to `ddg` gives 100%\nprecision, keeps 7 of 85 flows, and LOSES 21 OF THE 28 TRUE POSITIVES -- including\ncaddy's `for _, v := range os.Environ() { fmt.Println(v) }`, one of the three real\nleaks the earlier removal-authority trial found. Do not filter on confidence, and do\nnot treat analysis_method as a calibrated threshold.\n\nCLAIM-LEVEL correctness is roughly twice flow-level: 9 of 17 violated verdicts\n(52.9%) rest on at least one true flow. The headline a user reads first is more\ntrustworthy than the evidence row they drill into.\n\nFP MECHANISMS, named and counted: co-location-function 21, content-not-argument 8,\nreachability-only 8, co-location-file 7, control-dependence-only 7,\nmisattributed-source-read 2, unrelated-files 2, short-name-collision 1,\nopened-path-not-content 1. Plus 19 UNADJUDICABLE, all one mechanism.\n\nTHREE ITEMS FILED FROM THIS:\n  INV-rozob  taint source anchored to the <external> placeholder (19 flows; the row\n             count is sources x sinks, so it scales with the catalogue not the code)\n  INV-tutar  env_read -> host_secret means \"reads host description\" (GOOS, Getwd,\n             Executable, navigator.platform) while every claim written against it\n             means \"secret\"; host-secret-* is 48 of 85 flows at 22.9%\n  WI-palul   candidate cheap filter: sink call textually precedes the source read in\n             the same function body. Sized at 6 incidental instances; direction\n             DELETES findings, so measure both signs first.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT SUPPORT: precision, not accuracy -- nothing here measures recall.\nSix repos are not a random sample; pretix alone is 77% of the population and 14 of\nthe 28 true positives, mostly one Django read->InvoiceLine.objects.create shape. A\ncohort without a Django application would score lower."  # 9a1a
